      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, why a goblin? That's a weird comparison. \n\nAnyway, if it is the capacitor, how do I tell if it's actually blown just by looking at it? Is there a specific look to it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You’re right, the goblin thing just slipped out—I’ve got a bit of a reputation for that. Anyway, yes, a blown capacitor often has a telltale look: the top might be bulged or domed instead of flat, or you might see a small crack or oily residue leaking from the seam. Sometimes it’s subtle, though—a capacitor can fail electrically without any visible damage, so a multimeter is the only sure way to test it. If you see any swelling, leaking, or burn marks, that’s your smoking gun, but don’t assume it’s fine just because it looks clean. And remember, even a “dead” capacitor can hold a charge, so discharge it with an insulated screwdriver across the terminals before touching anything. That’s the kind of surprise that turns a simple fix into a trip to the ER—goblins have nothing on a capacitor’s bite.",
