      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay so if it looks bulged i should definitely replace it. once i get the new part, is there anything else that usually causes this? like could it be something in the wiring or just the part itself failing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A bulged capacitor is almost always the culprit, but it’s worth checking the wiring and the motor itself before you swap it out. Loose or corroded connections at the capacitor terminals can cause the same humming symptom, so make sure the spade connectors are snug and clean. Also, if the motor’s bearings are seized, a new capacitor won’t help—it’ll just hum again and burn out the new part. Give the fan blade a spin by hand (with power off) to confirm it moves freely; if it’s stiff or grinds, the motor needs attention too. In most cases, though, a bulged cap is the end of the story—just replace it, and you’re back to cool air. And if you’re ever unsure, a quick continuity test on the motor windings with a multimeter will tell you if it’s still healthy. That’s the goblin-proof way to avoid a second trip up the ladder.",
